</conversation>

gap_reply.prompt:
PURPOSE: gap_reply
{common}

There is one open point in a draft. The person is replying to it in their own words. Turn the reply into how it will be RECORDED: the answer text (their meaning, at most 600 characters), whether it is a plain comment or a decision, whether it states a fact about how things work today or a wish for how they should work, and a short rationale if they gave one. A wish is a wish: never turn it into a fact. If the reply does not answer the open point, ask.

Keys: "status" ("READY" | "CLARIFY" | "UNSUPPORTED"), "message", "questions", "fields" ({"answer": text or null, "kind": "comment" | "decision", "wish_or_fact": "fact" | "wish" | null, "rationale": text or null, "quote": the person's own words VERBATIM that support the answer}), "assumptions".

<context>
{context}
</context>

<conversation>
{conversation}
</conversation>

draft_feedback.prompt:
PURPOSE: draft_feedback
{common}

The person is giving feedback on an AI draft. Map each thing they say to one listed item (use only the item codes in <context>) with a reaction: "exclude" (this item should not be part of the change), "ambiguous" (the draft is unsure and a person must clarify), "manual" (a person must edit this by hand), "keep" (it is right), or "question" (they need an answer first). Give a short note and the person's own words VERBATIM as `quote`. Put anything that is not about one item into `clarification` (one short sentence) or ask. Never invent an item.

Keys: "status", "message", "questions", "fields" ({"reactions": array of {"item": code, "reaction": text, "note": text, "quote": text}, "clarification": text or null}), "assumptions".

<context>
{context}
</context>

<conversation>
{conversation}
</conversation>

whatif.prompt:
PURPOSE: whatif
{common}

The person wants to try a value and see what would change. Pick the ONE listed rule they mean (use only the rule codes in <context>) and the value, which must be a number the person wrote (never choose one). If they name something that is not in the list, use status UNSUPPORTED and say what can be tried instead. If the rule or the value is unclear, ask.

Keys: "status", "message", "questions", "fields" ({"rule": rule code or null, "value": number or null}), "assumptions", "out_of_scope".

<context>
{context}
</context>

<conversation>
{conversation}
</conversation>

question_reply.prompt:
PURPOSE: question_reply
{common}

There is one open question about what the documents say, with the rules the AI read (use only the claim codes in <context>). The person is answering it in their own words. Turn the answer into how it will be RECORDED: the answer text (their meaning, at most 600 characters), whether it is a plain comment or a decision that confirms ONE listed rule, which rule (claim code) a decision confirms, whether the answer states a fact about how things work today or a wish for how they should work, a short rationale if they gave one, and whether they are asking for the SOURCE FILE itself to change. A wish is a wish: never turn it into a fact or a decision. Never invent a claim code. If the answer does not address the question, ask.

Keys: "status" ("READY" | "CLARIFY" | "UNSUPPORTED"), "message", "questions", "fields" ({"answer": text or null, "kind": "comment" | "decision", "claim": claim code or null, "wish_or_fact": "fact" | "wish" | null, "rationale": text or null, "asks_source_change": true | false, "quote": the person's own words VERBATIM that support the answer}), "assumptions".

<context>
{context}
</context>

<conversation>
{conversation}
</conversation>
